The key to solving this question is to use the relation: \AtleastOneProb. Let $n$ be the number of independent random variables that we generate. Using the previous relation, we have that the probability that at least one of them is in the interval is equal to,
\begin{equation*}
    1 - P(\text{none of them are in )[0.6,0.7]} = 1 - P( X_{1) \notin [0.6,0.7], X_{2} \notin [0.6,0.7], ..., X_{n} \notin [0.6,0.7]}
\end{equation*}
where $X_{1}, ..., X_{n}$ are independent Unif$(0,1)$ random variables. Since each of the $X_{i}$ are independent, and since they're $U(0, 1)$, this is simply equal to,
\begin{align*}
    1 - P( X_{1) \notin [0.6,0.7], X_{2} \notin [0.6,0.7], ..., X_{n} \notin [0.6,0.7]} &= P( X_{1) \in [0.6, 0.7] }P( X_{2) \in [0.6, 0.7] }\dots P( X_{n) \in [0.6, 0.7] } \\
    &= 1 - 0.9^{n}
\end{align*}
The $0.9$ comes from the fact that the length of the interval is $0.7 - 0.6 = 0.1$, and since each $X_{i}$ is uniform on the interval $(0,1)$, we have $P(X_{i) \in [0.6, 0.7] } = \frac{0.1}{1} = 0.1$. From the question, we now want to find the smallest $n$ such that $1 - 0.9^{n} \geq 0.95$, which is equivalent to finding the smallest $n$ such that $0.9^{n} \leq 0.05$ by rearranging the inequality. Taking the logarithm of both sides yields,
\begin{align*}
    n \text{log}(0.9) &\leq \text{log}(0.05) \\
    n &\geq \frac{\text{log}(0.05)}{\text{log}(0.9)}
\end{align*}
Note that the last inequality is flipped since $\text{log}(0.9)$ is negative. Since $n$ needs to be a whole number, we have,
\begin{equation*}
    n = \text{ceil}\Big( \frac{\text{log}(0.05)}{\text{log}(0.9)} \Big) = 29
\end{equation*}

A good strategy for questions like this is to use counting methods to determine the probability. In this case we want the probability that the when the last white kitten is pulled out there are exactly three black kittens still in box. If we think about the sequence of kittens that we pull out and denote each black kitten as $B$ and each white kitten as $W$, then the probability will be the number of sequences of kittens that end in $WBBB$ divided by the total number of sequences of kittens.

$$$$

If a sequence of kittens ends with $WBBB$ then that means there are 11 remaining kittens to pull out of the box. Of these 11 kittens, 6 of them must be white. Thus, there are $\binom{11}{6}$ total kitten sequences that end in $WBBB$, since we need to fill 6 of the 11 spots with a white kitten. Similarly, if we want to determine the total number of kitten sequences, there are 15 spots in total and of these 15 we need to choose 7 of them to be a white kitten, so that there are $\binom{15}{7}$ total kitten sequences. Thus the final probability is,
\begin{equation*}
    \frac{\binom{11}{6}}{\binom{15}{7}} = \frac{14}{195}
\end{equation*}

