We will approach this question by conditioning the outcome on the first roll, and will follow the typical Markov state approach to finding the expectation. Define the following states: $S_{0} = $ Both dice show the same value, $S_{1} = $ The white die shows a value at least $2$ greater than the black die, and $S_{2} = $ The dice show different values, and the white die is not at least $2$ greater than the black die.

$$$$

Denoting our payout from the game as $V$, we then have
\begin{equation*}
    E[V] = P(S_{0)}E[V|S_{0]} + P(S_{1)}E[V|S_{1]} + P(S_{2)}E[V|S_{2]}
\end{equation*}
To get $P(S_0)$, we can simply note that no matter what the value of the first die is, the other die equals it with probability $1/8$, as one of the $8$ equally likely values is the one on the first die, which also takes each value with equal probability. Thus, $P(S_0) = 1/8$. 

$$$$

To get $P(S_1)$, the most straightforward, but time consuming, way is to simply enumerate all of pairs of rolls that have the white dice showing a value at least $2$ greater than the black dice. A more efficient way is to note that if the black dice is equal to some value, $k$, then the white dice can be any value between $k+2$ and $8$, inclusive. If $k \leq 6$, then there will be $8 - (k+2) + 1 = 7 - k$ numbers in between $8$ and $k$ that satisfy the condition. Since this amount of satisfactory values is dependent on $k$, we sum up over all values of $k$ to get
\begin{equation*}
    P(S_1) = \frac{1}{64}\sum_{k=1}^{6} (7-k) = \frac{21}{64}
\end{equation*}
Note that we don't need to consider $k = 7$ or $k = 8$, since there are no satisfactory values in these cases. Finally, we can simply get $P(S_2)$ by complementation of the other two conditions $1 - P(S_0) - P(S_1)$, which yields $P(S_2) = \frac{35}{64}$.

$$$$

As for the expected values, the key is to realize that after making a roll, the game either ends (in case we go to $S_{0}$), or it repeats itself. Keeping in mind our definitions of the states, we have,
\begin{align*}
    E[V|S_{0]} &= 0\\
    E[V|S_{1]} &= 1 + E[V] \\
    E[V|S_{2]} &= E[V]
\end{align*}

The addition of $1$ in $S_1$ is because we receive $\$1$, and the game repeats. Putting everything together yields,
\begin{align*}
    E[V] &= \frac{1}{8}(0) + \frac{21}{64}(1 + E[V]) + \frac{35}{64}(E[V]) \\
    &\rightarrow E[V] = \frac{21}{8}
\end{align*}

In order for the rolls to form a strictly increasing sequence, two things need to happen. The first is that none of the dice can show the same value, since otherwise the sequence would not be strictly increasing. The second is that the dice must be appropriately ordered, so that the first is lower than the second, the second is lower than the third, and the third is lower than the fourth. These events are not independent, so we can't just multiply their probabilities together. Instead what we will do is use the law of total probability and condition on the first event. We then have
\begin{align*}
    P(\text{Strictly increasing sequence)} &= P(\text{All dice are different)}P(\text{Strictly increasing sequence) | \text{All dice are different}} \\
    & + P(\text{All dice are not different)}P(\text{Strictly increasing sequence) | \text{All dice are not different}} \\
    &= P(\text{All dice are different)}P(\text{Strictly increasing sequence) | \text{All dice are different}} 
\end{align*}
The probability that all dice are different is $\frac{6\times5\times4\times3}{6^{4}}$, since if all the dice values are different, there are 6 values for the first dice, 5 values for the second dice, 4 values for the third dice, and 3 values for the fourth dice, and there are $6^{4}$ possible outcomes from rolling 4 dice. To find $P(\text{Strictly increasing sequence) | \text{All dice are different}}$, note that if all dice are different then there is exactly one ordering of those dice that forms a strictly increasing sequence. Since every sequence is equally likely, and since we are rolling 4 dice, this probability is equal to $\frac{1}{4!}$. Putting everything together, we thus have
\begin{align*}
    P(\text{Strictly increasing sequence)} &= P(\text{All dice are different)}P(\text{Strictly increasing sequence) | \text{All dice are different}} \\
    &= \frac{6\times5\times4\times3}{6^{4}} \times \frac{1}{4!} \\
    &= \frac{5}{432}
\end{align*}

In order for the product of the two rolls to be divisible by $6$ we must be able to write the product as $6k$, where $k$ is some integer. Since $2 \times 3 = 6$, any pair of dice rolls whose product contains a factor of $2$ and $3$ will be divisible by $6$. This means our possible pairs are $(3,4), (3,2), (4,3), (2,3),$ and any pair where one of the dice is a $6$, or both are $6$. In the case of one of the dice being a $6$, there are $10$ total possibilities since there are $5$ other possible values for the other dice, and $2$ ways to decide which of the two dice is $6$. In the case of both dice being $6$, there is only one possible pair. Since there are $6^{2} = 36$ total possible pairs for the dice rolls the final probability is,
\begin{equation*}
    P(\text{product of faces is divisible by 6)} = \frac{4 + 10 + 1}{36} = \frac{15}{36} = \dfrac{5}{12}
\end{equation*}

