In order for $5$ to be between our two randomly generated numbers, we must have that one number is less than $5$ and one number is greater than $5$. Denote our two randomly generated numbers as $X$ and $Y$. Using the law of total probability we have,
\begin{align*}
    P(5 \in [X,Y] ) &= P(X < 5)P(5 \in [X,Y] | X < 5) + P(X > 5)P(5 \in [X,Y] | X > 5) \\
    &= \frac{4}{9}P(5 \in [X,Y] | X < 5) + \frac{5}{9}P(5 \in [X,Y] | X > 5) \\
    & = \frac{4}{9}P(Y > 5) + \frac{5}{9}P(Y < 5) \\
    &= \frac{4}{9} \frac{5}{9} + \frac{5}{9}\frac{4}{9} \\
    &= \frac{40}{81}
\end{align*}
The third equality comes from the fact that, for example, given that $X < 5$, the only way that we can have $5 \in [X,Y]$ is if $Y > 5$.

