Since we are asked to calculate the probability of at least one of something, a natural step is to instead calculate the complement. That is, rather than calculating the probability of at least one sum being no greater than $3$, we will instead calculate $1$ minus the probability that none of the sums are no greater than $3$, which is just another way of saying we will calculate $1$ minus the probability all of the sums are strictly greater than $3$. 

$$$$

In order for the sum of the dice to be no greater than $3$, LeBron had to have gotten one of the following roll combinations: $(1,1), (2,1), (1,2)$. Since there are $6^{2} = 36$ possible roll pairs, the probability that the sum is no greater than $3$ is $\frac{3}{36}$, and thus, the probability that the sum is strictly greater than $3$ is $1 - \frac{3}{36} = \frac{33}{36}$. Since each pair of rolls is independent, the probability that all of the sums are strictly greater than $3$ is,
\begin{equation*}
    1 - \left(\frac{33}{36}\right)^{5} = \frac{87781}{248832} \approx 0.35
\end{equation*}

