Let the two cutpoints that Lewis' choose be denoted as $X_{1}$ and $X_{2}$, where $X_{1}$ is the cutpoint that is to the left of the midpoint of the piece of wood and $X_{2}$ is the cutpoint that is to the right of the midpoint of the piece of wood. Since they are chosen uniformly at random we have $X_{1} \sim U(0, \frac{1){2}}$ and $X_{2} \sim U(\frac{1, 2)}{1}$. We are thus interested in finding $P( X_{2) - X_{1} \leq \frac{1}{3}}$. Since we have two sources of randomness here, let's condition on one of them and use the law of total probability. This yields

\begin{align*}
    P( X_{2) - X_{1} \leq \frac{1}{3}} &= \int_{0}^{\frac{1}{2}} P( X_{2) - X_{1} \leq \frac{1}{3} | X_{1} = x} f_{X_{1}}(x) dx \\
    &= \int_{0}^{\frac{1}{2}} P( X_{2) - X_{1} \leq \frac{1}{3} | X_{1} = x} 2 dx \\
    &= \int_{0}^{\frac{1}{2}} P( X_{2) \leq \frac{1}{3} + X_{1} | X_{1} = x} 2 dx \\
    &= \int_{0}^{\frac{1}{2}} P( X_{2) \leq \frac{1}{3} + x} 2 dx \\
\end{align*}

which follows from the fact that $X_{1} \sim U(0, \frac{1){2}}$, and that $X_{1}$ and $X_{2}$ are independent.

