Since we know that $X_{2} \sim U(\frac{1, 2)}{1}$, we can easily see that $P( X_{2) \leq \frac{1}{3} + x} = 2(\frac{1}{3} + x - \frac{1}{2}) = 2(x - \frac{1}{6})$. It is tempting now to simply solve the integral. However, we must be careful with the bounds of integration. Note that since $X_{2}$ is always at least $\frac{1}{2}$, then $P( X_{2) \leq \frac{1}{3} + x} = 0$ for any $x$ such that $\frac{1}{3} + x < \frac{1}{2}$. Specifically, for any $x < \frac{1}{6}$. Thus, the lower bound on our integral is $\frac{1}{6}$, since the interior expression is equal to 0 from $[0,\frac{1}{6})$. We thus have,

\begin{align*}
    P( X_{2) - X_{1} \leq \frac{1}{3}} &=  \int_{\frac{1}{6}}^{\frac{1}{2}} 2\left(x - \frac{1}{6}\right) 2 dx \\
    &= \frac{2}{9}
\end{align*}

The outcome you need to repeatedly obtain on the coin is dependent on the first flip, as the two outcomes have different probability of occurrence per trial. Therefore, the key is to apply Law of Total Expectation and condition on the first flip.

$$$$

Let $L_1$ be the length of the first run and $H$ be the event that the first flip is heads. $H^c$ would be the event that the first flip is tails. By conditioning on $H$, $$E[L_1] = E[L_1 \mid H]P(H) + E[L_1 \mid H^c]P(H^c)$$ 

From the question, $P(H) = p$ and $P(H^c) = 1-p$. If the first flip is heads, the length of the first run is equal to the expected number of additional flips needed to obtain the first $\textbf{tails}$ (since the first tails will end our streak of heads) added to the $1$ head that already appeared.  Since we are looking at a discrete waiting time, the number of additional flips until the first tails is $T \sim \geom{1-p}$ distributed, since there is probability $1-p$ of tails per flip. However, there are $T-1$ heads that appear, as the last flip is the tails itself. The minus $1$ here cancels with the $1$ we added back above to account for the first flip, so the length of the first run given the first flip is heads is just 

\begin{equation*}
E[L_1 \mid H] = 1 +  E[T] - 1 = 1 + \frac{1}{1-p} - 1 =\frac{1}{1-p}
\end{equation*}

The same idea applies with the first flip being tails, except $1-p$ is now replaced with $p$, so $E[L_1 \mid T] = \frac{1}{p}$. Plugging these into the Law of Total Expectation yields $$E[L_1] = \dfrac{1}{1-p} \cdot p + \dfrac{1}{p} \cdot 1-p = \dfrac{p}{1-p} + \dfrac{1-p}{p}$$ This result makes intuitive sense, as it's symmetric about $1/2$ (the combinations with $p$ of heads and $1-p$ of tails is really just the same as $1-p$ of heads and $p$ of tails, up to relabeling the coin), and the expectation tends to $\infty$ at $0$ and $1$. With the specified values, the answer is $5/2$.

We can rewrite the probability expression as $P(V > Ux)$, which can be interpreted as an event in the plane. Since this is an event in the plane, it's best to integrate the joint PDF of $U$ and $V$ over this region. As $U$ is the minimum and $V$ is the maximum, we can use the formula of the joint PDF for order statistics to obtain their joint PDF. The formula for IID random variables with CDF $F(x)$ and PDF $f(x)$ is $$f(u,v) = \binom{n}{i-1,1,j-i-1,1,n-j}(F(u))^{i-1}f(u)(F(v) - F(u))^{j-i-1}f(v)(1 - F(v))^{n-j}$$ For the $U(0, 1)$ distribution, $f(x) = 1$ and $F(x) = x$. Using this with $i = 1$ and $j = n$, the joint PDF is $$f(u,v) = n(n-1)(v-u)^{n-2}I_{(0,1)}(v)I_{(0,v)}(u)$$ Plotting in the $(u,v)-$plane, the region of integration is a triangle vertices at $(0,0), (0,1),$ and $(1/x, 1)$. Performing the integration over the joint PDF, 

\begin{align*}
    P(V > Ux) &= \int_0^1 \int_0^{v/x} n(n-1)(v-u)^{n-2}~du~dv\\
    &= \int_0^1 n(v-u)^{n-1}\Big|_0^{v/x}~dv\\
    &= \int_0^1 nv^{n-1}\left(1 - \left(1 - \frac{1}{x}\right)^{n-1}\right)~dv\\
    &= 1 - \left(1 - \frac{1}{x}\right)^{n-1}
\end{align*}

With the specified values, the answer is $7/8$.

