    \item There are no runs of $n$ consecutive heads in the $f-n-2$ flips after the first and before the $(f-n-1)$st flips.
\end{itemize}

solution:
The most straightforward way to solve this question is to use the classic tail-sum formula for expectation. Note that this can be used since each $X_{i} \geq 0$, since they're $U(0, 1)$. Let $M =$ min$\{X_{1}, X_{2}, ..., X_{n}\}$. The tail sum formula in this case will be,
\begin{align*}
    E[M] = \int_{0}^{1} P(M \geq m) dm
\end{align*}
and now we just need to find $P(M \geq m)$, which is the probability that the minimum is above any value, $m$. 

$$$$

If the minimum of all of the $X_{i}$ is greater than some value $m$, then that implies every single $X_{i}$ is greater than $m$. Thus,
\begin{align*}
    P(M \geq m) &= P( X_{1) \geq m, X_{2} \geq m, ..., X_{n} \geq m} \\
    &= P( X_{1) \geq m}P( X_{2) \geq m}...P( X_{n) \geq m} \\
    &= (1-m) \times (1-m)  \times ... \times (1-m)  \\
    &= (1-m)^{n}
\end{align*}
The second line follows from the fact that each of the $X_{i}$ are independent, and the third line simply follows from the fact that each $X_{i} \sim U(0, 1)$.
$$$$

Returning to the tail-sum formula above we have,
\begin{align*}
    E[M] &= \int_{0}^{1} P(M \geq m) dm \\
    &= \int_{0}^{1} (1-m)^{n} dm \\
    &= \frac{1}{n+1}
\end{align*}
And with the specified value of $n=50$, we get the answer,
\begin{align*}
    E[M] = \frac{1}{51}
\end{align*}

Again letting $M=$ min $\{X_{1}, X_{2}, ..., X_{n}\}$, we have from the definition of expected value,
\begin{align*}
    E[M] = \int_{0}^{1} m f_{M}(m) dm
\end{align*}
And now our main task will be to figure out what $f_{M}(m)$, the density (pdf) of $M$, is. To do this we'll try the standard method of first finding the CDF, and then taking the derivative to find the density. The CDF of $M$ is,
\begin{align*}
    P(M < m) = 1 - P(M \geq m) = 1 - (1-m)^{n}
\end{align*}
which we showed how to solve for in the first solution. Thus the density of $M$ is,
\begin{align*}
    \frac{d}{dm} \left( 1 - (1-m)^{n} \right)= n m(1-m)^{n-1} = f_{M}(m)
\end{align*}
Plugging this in to the above definition of expected value we have,
\begin{align*}
    E[M] &= n \int_{0}^{1} m(1-m)^{n-1} dm \\
    &= n \int_{0}^{1} m(1-m)^{n-1} dm \\
    &= \frac{1}{n+1}
\end{align*}
And with the specified value of $n=50$, we of course get the answer,
\begin{align*}
    E[M] = \frac{1}{51}
\end{align*}

Note that this solution technique, while a little bit more involved than the first, is more general. The reason for this is that it doesn't require each $X_{i}$ to be non-negative, like in the first solution. That means that this solution technique can be used even if each $X_{i} \sim U(a, b)$ with $a < 0$.
